---

<Layout 
  title="CUTMAP: Comprehensive Utility for Thinning Mechanically via Automated Prescription"
  description="CUTMAP: A low-SWaP-C (~$4,439 Phase I BOM) under-canopy lidar-camera platform for single-tree forest mensuration and automated thinning prescriptions. USDA-NIFA STTR Phase I (#2025-70436-44908) by Spectral Sciences, Inc. and Oregon State University."
  image="/images/cutmap_cover_photo.jpg"
>

  <!-- SECTION 1: HERO BANNER & PROJECT PURPOSE -->
  <section class="relative pt-12 pb-16 overflow-hidden border-b border-border">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-b from-forest-950 via-background to-background pointer-events-none"></div>

    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 relative z-10">

      <!-- Sponsor, Company & Award Bar -->
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-4 mb-6">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
          <a 
            href="https://www.spectral.com" 
            target="_blank" 
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-3 bg-white hover:bg-slate-100 px-4 py-2 rounded-xl shadow-md border border-border/40 transition-all group"
            title="Visit Spectral Sciences, Inc. Official Website"
          >
            <img 
              src="/images/SSI_LOGO-transparent.png" 
              alt="Spectral Sciences, Inc. Logo" 
              class="h-6 sm:h-7 w-auto object-contain" 
            />
            <span class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-800 font-bold tracking-tight border-l border-slate-300 pl-3 hidden sm:inline">
              Spectral Sciences, Inc.
            </span>
          </a>

          <a 
            href="https://forestry.oregonstate.edu" 
            target="_blank" 
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2.5 bg-surface hover:bg-forest-900 px-3.5 py-2 rounded-xl shadow-md border border-border/60 hover:border-[#D73F09]/60 transition-all group"
            title="Academic Teaming Partner: Dr. Bogdan M. Strimbu, Oregon State University College of Forestry"
          >
            <span class="w-6 h-6 rounded-lg bg-[#D73F09]/15 border border-[#D73F09]/40 flex items-center justify-center text-[#D73F09] font-black text-xs font-mono">
              OSU
            </span>
            <div class="flex flex-col text-left">
              <span class="text-xs font-bold text-white group-hover:text-orange-400 transition-colors leading-tight">
                College of Forestry
              </span>
              <span class="text-[10px] font-mono text-text-dim leading-tight">
                Oregon State University &bull; Dr. Bogdan M. Strimbu
              </span>
            </div>
          </a>
        </div>

        <a 
          href="https://www.nifa.usda.gov/about-nifa/award-announcements/nifa-invests-20m-spur-small-business-led-innovation"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3.5 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-950/80 hover:bg-emerald-900 border border-emerald-500/50 hover:border-emerald-400 text-xs font-mono text-emerald-300 transition-all shadow-sm group"
          title="USDA-NIFA STTR Phase I Award #2025-70436-44908 (Topic 8.1 Forest and Related Resources)"
        >
          <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-emerald-400 animate-pulse"></span>
          <span>USDA-NIFA STTR Phase I (#2025-70436-44908) &bull; Spectral Sciences, Inc. &amp; Oregon State University</span>
          <svg class="w-3.5 h-3.5 text-emerald-400 group-hover:translate-x-0.5 transition-transform" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
            <path strokeLinecap="round" strokeLinejoin="round" strokeWidth="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14" />
          </svg>
        </a>
      </div>

      <!-- NEMO / SOMENS 2026 Conference Callout -->
      <div class="mb-6 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-forest-900/80 border border-accent/40 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 text-xs font-mono">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2.5 text-emerald-300">
          <span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-accent/20 text-accent font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-[10px]">NEMO / SOMENS 2026</span>
          <span>
            Presenting <strong class="text-white">"CUTMAP: A Low-SWaP-C Mobile Mapping Platform for Forest Mensuration"</strong> &bull; Tuesday, Nov 10, 2026 (10:45 AM)
          </span>
        </div>
        <a href="#field-campaigns" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light font-semibold underline">
          Jump to Phase I Results &darr;
        </a>
      </div>

      <!-- Main Headline, Subtitle & Lead Copy -->
      <div class="space-y-5">
        <div class="text-xs font-mono text-accent uppercase tracking-widest">
          Phase I of a Low-Cost Hardware and Software Prototype for Thinning Prescriptions
        </div>

        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-black text-white tracking-tight leading-[1.12]">
          CUTMAP: Comprehensive Utility for Thinning Mechanically via Automated Prescription
        </h1>

        <p class="text-lg sm:text-xl text-emerald-300 font-medium leading-relaxed max-w-4xl">
          A Low-SWaP-C Under-Canopy Lidar–Camera Platform for Single-Tree Forest Mensuration and Thinning Prescriptions
        </p>

        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm sm:text-base text-text-muted leading-relaxed max-w-4xl">
          <p>
            Forest thinning—whether commercial thinning to accelerate sawtimber yield or precommercial thinning to remove small-diameter wildfire ladder fuels—is decided <strong class="text-white">one tree at a time</strong>. Traditional fixed-radius cruise plots estimate stand-level averages, but cannot tell a forester or harvester operator which individual stem to cut or leave. Meanwhile, research mobile laser scanners achieve high accuracy (e.g., 1.93 cm DBH RMSE in Freißmuth <em>et al.</em> 2024) but carry hardware costs comparable to terrestrial laser scanners, which Mattamala <em>et al.</em> (2025) identify as the primary barrier to operational adoption.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">CUTMAP</strong> bridges the gap between low-cost edge sensors and quantitative silviculture. Developed by <strong class="text-white">Spectral Sciences, Inc. (SSI)</strong> and the <strong class="text-white">Oregon State University (OSU) College of Forestry</strong>, our Phase I proof-of-concept integrates a solid-state 3D lidar, 200 Hz IMU, dual global-shutter cameras, and an NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin (<strong class="text-white">~$4,439 Phase I component list price</strong>) to map understory stems at walking speed without GPS.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- CTAs -->
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 pt-2">
          <a 
            href="#field-campaigns" 
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3.5 rounded-xl bg-accent hover:bg-accent-light text-forest-950 font-bold text-sm font-mono transition-all shadow-lg hover:shadow-emerald-500/20 cursor-pointer"
          >
            <span>Explore Field Validation</span>
            <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path strokeLinecap="round" strokeLinejoin="round" strokeWidth="2.5" d="M14 5l7 7m0 0l-7 7m7-7H3" />
            </svg>
          </a>

          <a 
            href="#interactive-3d-viewer" 
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3.5 rounded-xl bg-surface hover:bg-forest-850 text-white border border-border hover:border-accent/40 font-semibold text-sm font-mono transition-all cursor-pointer"
          >
            <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-accent animate-pulse"></span>
            <span>Interactive 3D Point Cloud</span>
          </a>

          <a 
            href="#video-showcase" 
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-5 py-3.5 rounded-xl bg-forest-900/80 hover:bg-forest-800 text-text-muted hover:text-white border border-border text-sm font-mono transition-all"
          >
            <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-emerald-400" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 20 20">
              <path d="M6.3 2.841A1.5 1.5 0 004 4.11v11.78a1.5 1.5 0 002.3 1.269l9.344-5.89a1.5 1.5 0 000-2.538L6.3 2.84z" />
            </svg>
            <span>Watch Phase I Promo Video</span>
          </a>

          <a 
            href="#teaming-partnership" 
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-5 py-3.5 rounded-xl bg-forest-900/80 hover:bg-forest-800 text-text-muted hover:text-white border border-border text-sm font-mono transition-all"
          >
            <span>Phase II Teaming</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- KPI COUNTER GRID (SECTION 3 OF UPDATE GUIDE) -->
  <section class="py-16 bg-background border-b border-border">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6">
      
      <div class="text-center max-w-2xl mx-auto mb-10">
        <div class="text-xs font-mono text-accent uppercase tracking-widest mb-1">Verified Phase I Evidence</div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-white tracking-tight">Phase I Benchmarks at a Glance</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4 sm:gap-6">
        {kpis.map(kpi => (
          <div class="p-5 sm:p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-border hover:border-accent/40 transition-all flex flex-col justify-between">
            <div>
              <div class={`text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black font-mono ${kpi.color} mb-2 tracking-tight`}>
                {kpi.value}
              </div>
              <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold text-white mb-1">
                {kpi.label}
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="text-[11px] sm:text-xs text-text-muted mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-border/60 leading-snug">
              {kpi.detail}
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- INTERACTIVE 3D LIDAR STAND VIEWER -->
  <section id="interactive-3d-viewer" class="py-16 bg-surface/30 border-b border-border">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6">
      
      <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row md:items-end justify-between gap-4 mb-8">
        <div>
          <div class="text-xs font-mono text-emerald-300 uppercase tracking-widest mb-1">Live WebGL Visualization</div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-white tracking-tight">Interactive 3D Understory Scans (Burlington White Pine Groves)</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-text-muted max-w-md">
          Drag to orbit the 3D eastern white pine stands mapped with Livox Mid-360 lidar-inertial odometry. Switch between Groves 1–3 or toggle the height colormap.
        </p>
      </div>

      <CutmapLidarViewer client:load />

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 2: PLATFORM ARCHITECTURE & THE EMPIRICAL PIVOT TO LIDAR-INERTIAL ODOMETRY -->
  <section id="platform-architecture" class="py-16 bg-background border-b border-border">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6">
      
      <div class="mb-10">
        <div class="text-xs font-mono text-accent uppercase tracking-widest mb-1">Section 02 &bull; Platform Architecture &amp; Odometry</div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-white tracking-tight">
          Low-SWaP-C Hardware &amp; The Optical-to-Lidar Pivot
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-border flex flex-col justify-between">
          <div>
            <div class="text-xs font-mono text-accent font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Hardware Stack</div>
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-3">~$4,439 Phase I Prototype</h3>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
              A pulled utility cart carrying a <strong class="text-white">Livox Mid-360</strong> solid-state 3D lidar (200,000 pts/s, non-repetitive circular scan) with an integrated 200 Hz IMU, <strong class="text-white">two rear-facing FLIR Blackfly (<code>BFLY-U3-13S2C</code>)</strong> global-shutter color cameras on a 457.2 mm stereo baseline, a Garmin GLO 2 GNSS logger, and an <strong class="text-white">NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin Developer Kit</strong> (12 cores, 32 GB RAM, 275 TOPS, running ROS 2 Humble in MAXN mode), logging 63 field bags at an average capture rate of <strong class="text-white">28.96 MB/s (104 GB/h)</strong>.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="mt-4 pt-3 border-t border-border/60 text-[11px] font-mono text-text-dim">
            $2,440 sensors/battery + $1,999 Jetson Orin Dev Kit ($6,297 at Nov 2026 replacement prices)
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-border flex flex-col justify-between">
          <div>
            <div class="text-xs font-mono text-amber-300 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Empirical Pivot</div>
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-3">Why Below-Canopy Lidar Is Essential</h3>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
              Phase I explicitly tested whether passive cameras and an Extended Kalman Filter (EKF) could map the forest understory without lidar. Across three spring 2026 trials, pure stereo visual odometry collapsed in dense foliage (<strong class="text-white">0.00 m tracked</strong>, May 2026), monocular visual odometry suffered <strong class="text-white">23–98% scale drift</strong> against GPS, and the 15-state EKF proved unstable (April 2026). On July 24, 2026, the team pivoted positioning entirely to tightly coupled lidar-inertial odometry (<code>FAST-LIO2</code>), retaining the stereo cameras for semantic bark/foliage segmentation and optical silhouette mensuration.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="mt-4 pt-3 border-t border-border/60 text-[11px] font-mono text-text-dim">
            Livox Mid-360 + 200 Hz IMU (FAST-LIO2) + Optical Silhouette Fusion
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-border flex flex-col justify-between">
          <div>
            <div class="text-xs font-mono text-emerald-400 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Frame Alignment</div>
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-3">Stationary Gravity Levelling</h3>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
              Because <code>FAST-LIO2</code> initializes its coordinate frame from the starting IMU pose and never levels the map frame, small initial pitch/roll tilts produce large vertical excursions over hundreds of meters (a 220.9 m outdoor loop exhibited <strong class="text-white">7.23 m of vertical swing</strong> from frame tilt alone, reduced to 2.6 m after levelling). Integrating a brief stationary accelerometer gravity-vector alignment levels the local ground plane—collapsing ground elevation spread in Grove A from <strong class="text-white">0.52 m to 0.05 m</strong>—and achieves <strong class="text-white">0.010–0.037 m per 100 m closure error</strong> across all five indoor and outdoor loops started on a physical tape or grate mark.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="mt-4 pt-3 border-t border-border/60 text-[11px] font-mono text-text-dim">
            5 Marked Loops: 0.029 &amp; 0.013 m/100m (outdoor), 0.037 m/100m (indoor), 0.010 &amp; 0.019 m/100m (Mount Auburn)
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 3: MULTI-SENSOR CALIBRATION PHYSICS -->
  <section id="calibration-physics" class="py-16 bg-surface/30 border-b border-border">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6">
      
      <div class="mb-10">
        <div class="text-xs font-mono text-accent uppercase tracking-widest mb-1">Section 03 &bull; Multi-Sensor Calibration Physics</div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-white tracking-tight">
          Calibration Integrity: Unmasking Failure Coupling with an External Laser Scale Gate
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        <div class="p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-border">
          <div class="text-xs font-mono text-amber-300 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
            The Failure-Coupling Mechanism
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">
            2.50 mm Target Curl &rarr; 4.6% Focal Bias &rarr; ~140 mm Extrinsic Shift
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Low-cost multi-sensor platforms chain intrinsic camera calibration into stereo baseline estimation and finally into 3D lidar-to-camera extrinsic registration. During Phase I, a <strong class="text-white">2.50 mm peak-to-peak curl over a 320 mm span</strong> on a cardboard-backed checkerboard target biased the solved camera focal length <strong class="text-white">4.6% low</strong>.
          </p>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
            Standard internal camera calibration checks were completely blind to the defect (reprojection residuals remained under a pixel). When that focal error passed into the lidar-camera extrinsic solver, it forced a <strong class="text-white">133 mm lateral and ~140 mm fore-aft phantom shift</strong> in solved camera position.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-accent/40">
          <div class="text-xs font-mono text-accent font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
            Flat-Backed Target &amp; External Laser Scale Gate
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">
            0.66% Baseline Concordance &amp; 6.6 mm Extrinsic Centering
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Replacing the curled target with a rigid flat backing restored stereo baseline concordance to <strong class="text-white">454.20 mm vs. 457.2 mm physical tape (0.66% agreement, 0.309 px stereo RMS)</strong>.
          </p>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
            To prevent focal-scale coupling from passing undetected, we introduced an independent <strong class="text-white">external laser-rangefinder scale gate</strong> (lidar range slope <strong class="text-white">0.9964</strong> across 5 stations from 2–6 m; current stereo scale slope <strong class="text-white">0.9836 [−1.64%, SE ~0.95%]</strong> across 3 measured stations from 2–4 m at the 457.2 mm baseline) alongside <code>count^2.0</code> plane weighting that reduced lateral extrinsic centering error from <strong class="text-white">40.9 mm to 6.6 mm</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 4: FIELD CAMPAIGN 1 — 65 TAPED TREES & PARTIAL-ARC COVERAGE -->
  <section id="field-campaigns" class="py-16 bg-background border-b border-border">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 space-y-10">
      
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs font-mono text-accent uppercase tracking-widest mb-1">Section 04 &bull; Field Campaign 1 (Burlington, MA)</div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-white tracking-tight">
          Single-Tree Mensuration Physics: 65 Taped White Pine Stems
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-border">
          <div class="text-xs font-mono text-cyan-400 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Full-Ring Stems (&ge;340&deg;, n=42)</div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-2">+2.84 &plusmn; 1.46 cm (R&sup2; = 0.9836)</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
            Across three eastern white pine groves in Burlington, MA (65 stems taped at breast height, <code>z = 1.37 m</code>), stems observed over nearly complete walked rings (&ge;340&deg; angular sweep, <code>n = 42</code>) achieved <strong class="text-white">R&sup2; = 0.9836 and RMSE 3.18 cm</strong>, consisting of a nearly constant <strong class="text-white">+2.84 cm outer-bark offset</strong> and a tight <strong class="text-white">&plusmn;1.46 cm spread</strong> (41 of 42 stems read slightly high because lidar pulses reflect from outer bark ridges and lichens). Across <strong class="text-white">all 65 taped stems</strong> regardless of angular coverage, agreement was <strong class="text-white">+3.26 &plusmn; 2.53 cm (RMSE 4.12 cm, R&sup2; = 0.951)</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-border">
          <div class="text-xs font-mono text-amber-300 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Partial-Arc Coverage Effect</div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-2">+2.79 cm per 100&deg; Missing Arc</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
            Trail-side walks and harvesting machines view most trunks from one side. Across the 65 taped stems, diameter error varied systematically with the visible arc angle at a slope of <strong class="text-white">&minus;2.791 cm per 100&deg; of coverage</strong>—meaning a stem viewed from one side reads <strong class="text-white">+2.79 cm wider for every 100&deg; of missing trunk arc</strong> (<code>R&sup2; = 0.273, p = 8.2 &times; 10⁻⁶</code>).
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-border">
          <div class="text-xs font-mono text-emerald-300 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Lidar-Gated Optical Mensuration</div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-2">+1.92 &plusmn; 3.44 cm (n=52, Pooled)</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
            When the lidar point cloud certifies that the line of sight to a trunk is unobstructed, prompting the Segment Anything Model (<code>SAM ViT-B</code>) with projected 3D lidar trunk priors recovers single-viewpoint optical DBH at <strong class="text-white">+1.92 &plusmn; 3.44 cm (n = 52, pooled across both cameras)</strong>—bypassing the partial-arc circle-fitting inflation from a single viewpoint.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Verified Figures for Burlington White Pine Groves -->
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        <figure class="rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-border overflow-hidden flex flex-col justify-between">
          <div class="p-3 bg-black/40">
            <img 
              src="/images/cutmap/fig_multi_stand_stem_maps.png" 
