  <!-- SECTION 6: FIELD CAMPAIGN 3 — HARVARD FOREST SMITHSONIAN FORESTGEO BENCHMARK (HF253) -->
  <section id="harvard-forest" class="py-16 bg-background border-b border-border">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 space-y-8">
      
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs font-mono text-accent uppercase tracking-widest mb-1">Section 06 &bull; Field Campaign 3 (Petersham, MA)</div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-white tracking-tight">
          Natural Mixed-Forest Benchmark &amp; Double Sampling: Harvard Forest (HF253)
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-border">
          <div class="text-xs font-mono text-accent font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">3.26 km Trail Network</div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-2">87.0% Recall / 93.1% Precision (&ge;10 cm DBH)</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
            On September 24, 2026, CUTMAP surveyed <strong class="text-white">3,264 m of forest trails across 17 walks (65 minutes, 779M lidar points, 113 GB)</strong> inside the 35-hectare Smithsonian ForestGEO plot at Harvard Forest (<code>HF253</code>), covering <strong class="text-white">4.39 hectares</strong> within 15 m of the trail network. On a sealed 12-cell benchmark scored once against reviewed scan labels, the detector achieved <strong class="text-white">87.0% recall and 93.1% precision on merchantable stems (&ge;10 cm DBH)</strong>, and <strong class="text-white">72.0% recall / 86.2% precision across all stems (&ge;1 cm)</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-border">
          <div class="text-xs font-mono text-amber-300 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Size-Dependent Diameter Error</div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-2">+0.2 cm on Large Sawtimber (&ge;40 cm)</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
            Against census-matched stems, median diameter bias decreased monotonically with tree size: <strong class="text-white">+0.2 cm</strong> on large sawtimber (&ge;40 cm DBH, <code>n=14</code>), <strong class="text-white">+2.4 cm</strong> on 20–40 cm stems (<code>n=32</code>), <strong class="text-white">+4.8 cm</strong> on 10–20 cm poles (<code>n=30</code>), and <strong class="text-white">+14.9 cm</strong> on 1–10 cm saplings (<code>n=48</code>, where 2–3 cm lidar beam divergence and range noise inflate small-ring fits).
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-accent/40">
          <div class="text-xs font-mono text-emerald-400 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Two-Phase Double Sampling</div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-2">45.98 [40.19, 52.30] m&sup2;/ha vs. 47.44 Truth</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
            Across 441 grid cells (4.41 ha), the uncorrected lidar map overestimated stand basal area by 57% (74.34 vs. <strong class="text-white">47.44 m&sup2;/ha census truth</strong>). Applying classical <strong class="text-white">two-phase double-sampling ratio estimation</strong> calibrated with 40 reference cells removed the bias and recovered stand basal area at <strong class="text-white">45.98 [40.19, 52.30] m&sup2;/ha</strong> (held-out transfer test: <strong class="text-white">45.71 [39.17, 53.35] m&sup2;/ha</strong> vs. 47.32 m&sup2;/ha truth). Critically, because 40 reference cells alone yielded <strong class="text-white">45.63 [39.94, 51.44] m&sup2;/ha</strong>, Phase I revealed that diameter noise on small stems (&lt;10 cm) currently limits cell-level basal-area correlation (<code>&rho; &approx; 0.37</code>), defining the exact small-stem camera-fusion requirement for Phase II.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

